  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Why Server Drives Use SAS",
  "hero.sub": "The same platters are built as SATA and as SAS drives.<br>Why do storage enclosures in data centres insist on SAS?",
  "hero.lead": "An enclosure holds dozens or hundreds of drives, and behind them sit two controllers. The rules are strict: if any cable or board fails, the data must still be readable; and if what comes back is wrong, someone must notice rather than pass it on silently. We go through SAS one layer at a time: which command language it speaks, how many connections one read opens on the wire, how two ports cut downtime from an hour to a few minutes, how one controller reaches a hundred drives through an expander, why a four-lane wide port cannot saturate one drive, why a SATA drive fits into a SAS bay, what the 8 extra bytes after every 512 bytes protect against, and why 24G needs error correction. <strong>Each model is either a real rule from the standard or a published result</strong>, scaled down so it runs in a web page.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The numbers from the standards (command formats, frame lengths, encodings, check polynomials) come from T10 proposal documents, the Seagate SCSI command reference and vendor data sheets; the sources are listed at the foot of the page. <strong>Where a value is marked as a “tuned parameter”, it was chosen so the model can be computed, not measured</strong>; the text says how far the conclusion moves when that value is halved or doubled. This page covers mechanisms and specifications only, and <strong>does not rate any drive or card as a purchase</strong>. For the platters and flash themselves, see <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”</a>; for SATA encoding and frames, see <a href='../how-sata-works/'>“Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”</a>; for NVMe, see <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”</a>.",

  "toc.ch1": "Where SAS sits on the map",
